@section board_stm32vldiscovery_configs Build configurations

| Preset | Configuration | Clock setup | Use |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `stm32vldiscovery` | `configs/stm32vldiscovery_defconfig` | PLL to 24 MHz | Physical board |
| `stm32vldiscovery-qemu` | same defconfig + overlay `src/board/stm32vldiscovery/qemu.yaml` | Skipped | QEMU only |

```bash
cmake --preset stm32vldiscovery-qemu -DPython3_EXECUTABLE="$PWD/.venv/bin/python"
cmake --build --preset stm32vldiscovery-qemu
bash scripts/run-qemu-stm32vldiscovery.sh
python3 tests/qemu_files_test.py --board stm32vldiscovery
```

QEMU maps its first serial port to USART1. Exit the interactive session with
Ctrl-A, then X.

@warning Do not flash the QEMU build to a physical board. It skips clock setup,
so the board would run at the 8 MHz reset clock while assuming 24 MHz.

@section board_stm32vldiscovery_clock Clock

With `clock_setup` true (the default in `board.yaml`), `board_init()` runs the PLL
from HSI/2 &times; 6 = 24 MHz, the STM32F100 maximum, with undivided AHB, APB1,
and APB2 clocks. Each step waits with a bounded iteration budget; a failure
halts before the console starts, so diagnose it with a debugger.

QEMU does not model the clock controller or GPIO: reads return 0 and writes are
ignored. Its CPU and SysTick clock is fixed at 24 MHz, so the QEMU overlay
(`qemu.yaml`, `clock_setup: false`) skips clock setup and the board reports
24 MHz in both configurations.

@section board_stm32vldiscovery_memory Memory budget

@htmlinclude[block] stm32vldiscovery-memory.svg

Both defconfigs change four defaults to fit 8 KB of RAM:

| Setting | Default | This board |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `CONFIG_HOMECORE_KERNEL_MAIN_STACK_SIZE` | 3072 | 2048 |
| `CONFIG_HOMECORE_SHELL_BASIC_MAX_NESTING` | 8 | 4 |
| `CONFIG_HOMECORE_VFS_MAX_FILE_SIZE` | 4096 | 1024 |
| `CONFIG_HOMECORE_KERNEL_STDIO_BUFFERED` | y | n (saves about 2 KB of heap) |

VFS entries and descriptors use heap only while they exist, so the default caps
of 16 files, 16 directories, and 16 open files are kept. In QEMU, about 450
bytes of heap are used after boot, and 16 directories plus 16 empty files add
about 2 KB. File contents, BASIC programs, and variables share the remaining
heap. When it runs out, creating an entry fails with "Not enough space"
(`ENOMEM`), which differs from "No space left on device" at a cap.

BASIC lines may nest parentheses, unary signs, function calls, and
`IF ... THEN` up to 4 levels. Measured stack peaks in QEMU are about 0.9 KB for
file commands and 1.7 KB for BASIC at the nesting limit, leaving about 380 bytes
of margin. `mem` reports heap use and the stack high-water mark.

@section board_stm32vldiscovery_console Console wiring (physical board)

@htmlinclude[block] stm32vldiscovery-wiring.svg

| Board pin | Adapter pin |
| --- | --- |
| PA9 (USART1 TX) | RX |
| PA10 (USART1 RX) | TX |
| GND | GND |

Use a 3.3 V USB-to-UART adapter at 115200 baud, 8N1, no flow control. The
on-board ST-LINK/V1 has no virtual COM port. OpenOCD provides
`board/stm32vldiscovery.cfg` for it; on Linux the ST-LINK/V1 needs the
`usb-storage` quirk `483:3744:i`. These flashing steps have not been checked
with this port:

```bash
openocd -f board/stm32vldiscovery.cfg \
  -c "program build/stm32vldiscovery/homecore verify reset exit"
```

@section board_stm32vldiscovery_validation Hardware validation checklist

Confirm the banner, `uptime` against elapsed time (which checks the 24 MHz
PLL), `mem`, the file commands, `basic`, and `reboot`, as described in the
[porting guide](https://github.com/grzegorz-grzeda/home-core/blob/main/docs/porting.md#validation).

The full board guide is
[docs/boards/stm32vldiscovery.md](https://github.com/grzegorz-grzeda/home-core/blob/main/docs/boards/stm32vldiscovery.md).
*/
